text:
Jamie (SE): Heads up — HeliosBank POC request landed. They want SSE-style streaming for responses, function/tool calls to a downstream payment gateway (signed webhook), and strict structured JSON output (they'll validate against a schema). Also constraints: on-prem/reserved deployment, SAML SSO, HSM-backed KMS, and audit log retention requirements. Can we confirm recommended SDK flow + any pricing delta for reserved/Private setup?

Alex W: Quick clarifying Qs — what's the expected QPS and max tokens per interaction? And are the tool calls only to their gateway (we proxy) or do they expect Redwood to call third-party endpoints?

Jamie (SE): Peak ~120 QPS, avg ~40. Typical prompt+response <= ~2k tokens. Tool calls are to their payment gateway; they want signatures and short-lived creds for each call (ephemeral).

Maria G: For pricing, Dedicated/Private will be the route. For sustained 120 QPS we should estimate reserved capacity — rough ballpark 8–10 A100-class GPUs, but I'll run the usage model and ping finance for an accurate quote. Also need to surface any add-on fees for extended audit retention.

Samir: SDK/implementation notes: use the streaming endpoint with function/schema metadata and prefix caching for repeated payment routing prompts. Example Node streaming + function-call pattern (minimal):

```javascript
const res = await fetch("https://api.redwood.ai/v1/streams?model=redwood-prod", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Authorization": "Bearer $REDWOOD_KEY", "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    input: "route_payment",
    functions: [
      {
        name: "call_gateway",
        description: "Invoke customer payment gateway",
        parameters: { type: "object", properties: { amount: { type: "number" }, id: { type: "string" } }, required: ["amount","id"] }
      }
    ],
    json_schema: { type: "object", properties: { status: { type: "string" }, tx_id: { type: "string" } }, required: ["status"] }
  })
});
for await (const chunk of res.body) { process.stdout.write(chunk); }
```

Samir: Key implementation tips: 1) Use KV/prefix caching for repeated templates, 2) enable continuous batching at the runtime layer to improve unit cost without blowing latency SLOs, 3) sign webhook payloads with short-lived keys generated via their KMS rotation policy.

Jamie (SE): Do we have an off-the-shelf pattern for integrating customer KMS/HSM for signing and short-lived creds, or is that an SRE/security project?

Alex W: We support AWS KMS and CloudHSM integrations in Private mode; for a presales POC we can mock rotation with ephemeral tokens, but for production HSM-backed signing we'll need Security to approve the onboarding and a compliance addendum. I'll coordinate.

DevEx Bot: :memo: Presales checklist link: https://redwood.internal/presales/runbooks/sidecar-sdks — includes sample repos, SSO checklist, and security questionnaire template.

Maria G: I'll take SAML/Security questionnaire and prepare the SOX/SOC2 answers they asked for. Also confirm IP allowlist & audit log retention options (30/90/365 days) with infra.

Jamie (SE): Can someone stand up a slim demo repo (Node + Rust example) that shows streaming + function call + structured output + ephemeral creds? Need to hand it over to the customer by EOD tomorrow for their internal review. Also need a preliminary cost estimate for 3-month pilot (dedicated).

Alex W: :thumbsup: I'll draft the demo repo and sample config (k8s helm values for reserved). Maria — can you get the cost estimate?

Maria G: On it. Will share a spreadsheet with reserved GPU and SRE-onboarding line items in ~4 hours.

Samir: I'll paste a tiny Rust example into the repo and add a short README on how to wire in json_schema enforcement + function-call hooks.

Jamie (SE): Appreciate it team. If Security needs anything from the customer (e.g., proof of HSM compliance), say so and I'll request it.

Alex W: Noted. I'll loop in Security and Eng-Platform after Maria posts the estimate.

Samir: FYI — small runtime note: if they insist on sub-100ms token latency under 120 QPS, we may need to adjust batching windows or add burst capacity. Will call that out in the repo README.

Jamie (SE): Great — thanks everyone.
